00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:48.980
Now let us move into the strategy spotlight on screening for high ROIC compounders.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:54.680
Return on invested capital measures how efficiently a company turns capital into profits

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:57.060
after accounting for the cost of that capital.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.980
In today's environment of constrained consumer spending and selective AI demand,

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:09.640
focusing on high ROIC businesses helps filter names that can compound even when macro data weekends.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:16.440
The approach starts with screening for companies posting ROIC above 15% over five years,

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:20.340
then checking free cash flow consistency and competitive positioning.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:27.440
Platforms such as wealth simple or quest rate allow quick ROIC polls via their fundamental data tabs.

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:31.940
Cross check against the most recent quarterly filings before committing capital.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:38.720
This screen worked best during 2018 to 2019 and 2022 to 2023 periods,

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:42.960
when broad multiples compressed yet quality compounders still delivered.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:48.580
The main risk is paying too high a multiple for already recognized ROIC leaders,

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:53.420
so always pair the metric with a margin of safety check on forward earnings.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:03:00.100
Canadian investors can run this filter inside a TFSA without triggering frequent trading concerns

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:02.740
when the holding period stays at least several weeks.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:09.140
If you are using quest rate, export the results into a simple spreadsheet and rank by five-year

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:14.100
revenue growth next, that extra step adds discipline without extra cost.

